Comment (collector.go:310):
`collectELBV2` appends the same `lbErrors` slice to both `errors["target"]` and every per-load-balancer `errors[lbARN]`. Since `collectTarget` later joins `allCollectionErrors(collected.errors)`, a single AWS call failure can be duplicated N times (once per load balancer), inflating the returned error and making logs noisy. Consider keeping call-level errors only once (e.g., under `"target"`) and reserving per-resource error slices for resource-specific failures (tags, per-TG health), or deduplicating before joining.
---
Comment (collector.go:330):
Same duplication pattern for target groups: `tgErrors` are appended to `errors["target"]` and to every `errors[tgARN]`, which can multiply a single `DescribeTargetGroups` failure by the number of target groups when `allCollectionErrors` is joined. Recommend storing collection-call errors once and/or deduplicating before joining into the top-level `Collect` error.

---
Comment (collector.go:568):
`matchEventsToLoadBalancers` is O(events × loadBalancers × listeners) due to nested loops and repeated `eventMentions` string scans over raw CloudTrail JSON. This can become expensive in accounts with many LBs/listeners or larger lookback windows. Consider indexing once (e.g., build maps from listenerARN->lbARN and lbARN/lbID sets, then for each event check its resource list / parsed requestParameters for a single match) to reduce repeated scans and avoid quadratic growth.

---
Comment (README.md:207):
The coverage section says `DescribeTags` is only used for load balancer tags, but the implementation calls `DescribeTags` for listeners and target groups as well (and tests assert listener/target-group tags are populated). Either update this bullet to reflect that tags are collected for multiple ELBv2 resource types, or adjust collection to match the documented scope.

---
Comment (.github/workflows/build-and-upload.yml:37):
The tag sanitization used to build the OCI image tag (`clean=... | tr ...`) no longer enforces Docker/OCI tag constraints like max length (128 chars) and a leading `[A-Za-z0-9_]`. This can produce invalid tags for unusual refs, causing release failures. Consider truncating to 128 chars and prefixing a safe character when the tag would start with `.` or `-` (or reuse the previous SAFE_TAG logic).
---
Comment (.github/workflows/build-and-upload.yml:38):
`prerelease` is inferred from `GITHUB_REF_NAME` containing a hyphen. Many non-prerelease tags/versions (or organizational tag schemes) can include `-`, which would incorrectly skip publishing the `:latest` tag. Prefer deriving prerelease from the GitHub Release metadata (when available) or performing a stricter semver prerelease check instead of a simple substring match.
---
Comment (LICENSE:6):
The `LICENSE` file appears to be a short paraphrase of Apache 2.0 rather than the full license text. Repositories typically include the complete, unmodified Apache License 2.0 terms (sections 1–9 plus the appendix) to avoid ambiguity and ensure compliance. Consider replacing this with the standard full Apache 2.0 license text.
---
Comment (.github/workflows/build-and-upload.yml:22):
The GoReleaser action step no longer pins the GoReleaser CLI version (the previous workflow used `version: '~> v2'`). If the action defaults to `latest`, releases can become non-deterministic and may break when GoReleaser introduces changes. Consider explicitly setting the `version` input (or pinning to an exact GoReleaser version) for reproducible builds.

